\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Before we begin, a quick reminder\'97you can choose the depth you want to go into. If you don\'92t want to answer something, just let us know. And if anything comes up\'97no problem, we can stop or skip questions anytime.\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Yeah, I feel comfortable with that. I can stop or skip if I want.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Great. The conversation has two parts. The first one is more personal\'97about your experience, your past and present. The second is more about the format of the product and technical details.\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Sounds good.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Let\'92s start with some simple background questions. How old are you, where do you live, and what are your main priorities right now?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  My name is Prafda. I\'92m 33 years old, and I live in Chiang Mai with my daughter. My main goal right now is stability\'97for myself and for my baby. That\'92s my focus for this stage of life.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Have you heard about attachment styles before?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Yes, I\'92ve looked into it a bit. I don\'92t know if I fully understand it the right way, but from what I know, attachment is something we learn from a very young age. It\'92s part of being human\'97a necessary part for survival. But how we learn it depends on each family, each environment. That\'92s the base of any relationship.\
From my marriage, I started to explore this more deeply\'97asking myself what\'92s really happening between us. Why does the relationship feel this way? I discovered that what we had was probably an anxious\'96avoidant dynamic. That led me to look deeper, especially at myself\'97why I react the way I do.\
I started reflecting on my childhood and realized how I was conditioned growing up. That conditioning shaped how I behave and feel in relationships, especially in my marriage.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  So during your marriage you discovered attachment theory, and it helped you understand your connection with your husband better\'97to see what was really going on?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Yes, exactly. Mostly it helped me understand 
\f2\i myself
\f1\i0 . I began questioning myself more than focusing on him.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  So you identify your attachment style as anxious?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Yes.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Was it always like that, even before you knew about attachment theory?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Yes. I was always anxious, worried, controlling\'97filled with insecurities. I didn\'92t know how to name it then, but it was always there. In my mind, I constantly questioned love, questioned actions.\
And honestly, he was my first real love, my first relationship by my own choice. I always needed to question him\'97\'93Does he really love me? Why did he do that? What does it mean?\'94 It\'92s been my pattern: assuming there\'92s something hidden behind even his kind gestures.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  So on the mind level, it\'92s like a constant search for reasons, for explanations?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Yes. Trying to make sense of everything\'97even the good things\'97instead of just appreciating them.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  How does that feel emotionally or physically? Could you describe it\'97what happens in your body or emotions when it comes up?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Even now, I can\'92t always separate what\'92s mental and what\'92s emotional. I\'92m very sensitive, but sometimes I don\'92t know what level it\'92s coming from. When something good happens\'97like he says, \'93I love you, you\'92re the only one I\'92ve ever wanted\'94\'97my first reaction is warmth in my chest, happiness, feeling lucky.\
But immediately after, my mind jumps in trying to 
\f2\i prove
\f1\i0  whether it\'92s true or not. I start analyzing, searching for reasons to convince myself it\'92s 
\f2\i not
\f1\i0  true. It\'92s like a loop: part of me wants to believe, but another part is sure it\'92s a lie. I don\'92t know if it\'92s about trust between us or something deeper inside me.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Yes, I know that feeling. Even when something good happens, there\'92s that anxious voice saying, \'93This can\'92t be real, I don\'92t deserve it.\'94\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Exactly. But it also began after the trust was broken. Before that, during the first year of the relationship, there was peace. I remember feeling calm, not worried about being abandoned or cheated on. But after discovering certain things, that peace disappeared\'97and anxiety took over.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  So your anxiety came after specific events\'97it wasn\'92t there from the start?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Yes, that\'92s true. In the beginning, my only fear was that he wouldn\'92t take me seriously, that he\'92d flirt and leave. I was scared he wouldn\'92t commit or marry me. But after we got married and I found out things, the whole anxiety pattern began.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  When anxiety or worry shows up, how do you usually deal with it?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Until recently\'97maybe three months ago\'97I used to control everything: his phone, his work, where he went, what he did. I felt I had to prove to myself what was true. It gave short-term relief, but it pulled me deeper into darkness.\
After one session with my therapist, I started to understand what control really was\'97how insecurity and acceptance work. And when he cut off my access to his private life, I suddenly felt more peace. Not knowing everything turned out to be calmer.\
My mind still wants to know, but deep inside there\'92s more space. I realized I can\'92t control his actions; whatever I do, it\'92s his choice. But I can control my 
\f2\i own
\f1\i0  world\'97how I feel, how I respond. That\'92s what I\'92m focusing on now.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  So you\'92re shifting from trying to control 
\f2\i him
\f1\i0  to regulating your own state and boundaries.\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Exactly. Before, all my energy went toward him. Now I turn that energy toward myself\'97my state, my actions. That\'92s what truly changes my emotional landscape.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Have you used any particular practices or tools to help with that?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  At first it was support from friends\'97just talking so my mind didn\'92t explode. My daughter also keeps me grounded; I can\'92t disappear into anxiety when I have to be present for her.\
And therapy, even one session, helped name things clearly. Plus, I pray\'97I talk to God. Faith and time heal. I can feel how much I\'92ve grown in the last four months.\
